{
	"description": "Hyperliquid testnet, 2026-10-13. Does Hyperliquid judge the signatures of a multi-sig request over the inner action as it was sent, or over its own writing of it? Eight requests through a 2-of-3 lab multi-sig, each a vault deposit of 1 USDC, which is under the minimum: every one is refused and nothing moves, and which refusal comes back says whether the signatures were accepted. `posted` is the form of the inner action in the body, `innerSignedOver` the form the two signers signed, `outerSignedOver` the form the leader signed the envelope over. The forms: `canonical` (type, vaultAddress, isDeposit, usd; the address in lowercase), `reordered` (the same fields in the order usd, isDeposit, vaultAddress, type), `capitals` (the canonical order with the address's hex digits in capitals). Result: signatures made over the canonical form are accepted whatever form was posted (\"Vault deposits must be at least $5.\" is the answer of a request whose signatures passed); signatures made over any other form are refused, the leader's first (\"Invalid multi-sig outer signer\"), the signers' when the leader's is canonical (\"Invalid multi-sig inner signer\"). So Hyperliquid reads the request, writes the inner action out in its own form, and hashes that. Not measured here: a number's trailing zeros (a vault action has no decimal string).",
